[UK.Description]
Battle for Caen
[UK.Epilogue]
Operation Epsom yielded mixed results. The British army failed to turn the tide in the battle for Caen, and suffered considerable losses in the attempt. However, at the same time, the Germans had to divert all their armoured forces in the area into this battle, if they were to hold off the attack of the British, and their losses were every bit as heavy. Considering that the Allies could replenish their troops much faster, Operation Epsom contributed significantly to the eventual defeat of the Germans in Normandy. The results of the Operation Goodwood were equally mixed. Although they breached the enemy’s defences and captured the area, British mobile forces could win sufficient room to manoeuvre, and the British suffered heavy losses across the board, especially in tanks. But at the same time they demonstrated to the Germans that a combination of air raids and tank attacks could breach even the strongest of German defences. This led the Germans to believe that the British were the most dangerous of their enemies, and as a result, the Germans concentrated most of their armoured forces against the British lines. Thus, when Operation Cobra began, and the Americans attacked, the Germans simply did not have enough troops to repel them.
[UK.FullName]
Battle for Caen
[.Description]
[.FullName]
[Cheux.Description]
Operation Epsom commences. Supported by tanks, units of the 46th Infantry Brigade attacked and, after a fierce battle, captured the village of Cheux.
[Cheux.FullName]
Operation Epsom
[Cagny.Description]
Continuing their advance, the 11th Armoured Division attempted to capture the village of Cagny, but retreated after suffering heavy losses inflicted by 88mm air defence guns.
[Cagny.FullName]
Cagny
[Brass.Description]
In the afternoon of July 19, the 11th Armoured Division attacked the village of Bras, ultimately capturing it over the course of a pitched battle with the 1st SS Division.
[Brass.FullName]
Bras
[Four.Description]
A part of the 11th Armoured Division pressed the advance southward, reaching the outskirts of the village of Four. After a raging battle, the British managed to secure the locality.
[Four.FullName]
Four
[Cuverville.Description]
In the morning of July 18, 1944, Operation Goodwood commenced on the heels of an air raid. The 11th Armoured Division engaged the 16th Luftwaffe Field Division, breached its defences, and captured the village of Cuverville.
[Cuverville.FullName]
Operation Goodwood
[Hill112.Description]
In the morning of June 28, the 29th Armoured Brigade of the 11th Armoured Division attacked and captured the strategic Hill 112. The 12th SS Panzer Division counterattacked immediately. Although they failed to defeat the British, they nevertheless inflicted heavy losses.
[Hill112.FullName]
Hill 112
[Tourmaville.Description]
In the afternoon of June 27, in the course of pitched battle, the 29th Armoured Brigade of the 11th Armoured Division captured the village of Tourmauville, crossed the Odon river, and established a bridgehead.
[Tourmaville.FullName]
River Crossing
[Mondrainville.Description]
Having captured the village of Cheux, the 11th Armoured Division pressed the advance southward, eliminating all German strong points along their route. However, the Germans put up fierce resistance, and launched multiple tank counterattacks. They managed to inflict heavy losses on the British armoured forces, and thus blunt the spear-tip of the British advance.
[Mondrainville.FullName]
Pressing South
